From: uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com (Finn Lindgren)
Date: Mon, 04 Jun 2018 11:17:48 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] What does "max.logdens= -1.#IND00" mean?
Message-ID: <yGvxXxCX0S0.a-c5EoptAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <yGvxXxCX0S0.6RtYtMBhAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <yGvxXxCX0S0.i4NtXtTWAgAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com> <yGvxXxCX0S0.6RtYtMBhAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/yGvxXxCX0S0/m/a-c5EoptAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Technically speaking, #IND00 is a NaN value ("indeterminate"), and
infinity would be #INF00, but the root cause would be similar;
  sqrt(negative), log(negative), 0/0 all produce various NaN:s (the
IEEE standard has more than one NaN...)
  1/0 produces +Inf
  (-1)/0 produces -Inf

And I concur with both Haavard and Haakon; an initial run with more
informative priors helps to find a better starting point for the
"real" run.  Also, user-specified priors may have probability mass
away from the default initial optimisation starting point, so when
specifying priors, do check that the (default or use-specified)
initial value makes sense...

Finn

